    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d3">

                <pb facs="0116" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg2"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">857h.01/21½</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of State of a Conversation
                        with the French Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Jusserand</hi>),
                        <hi rendition="#italic">June 21, 1924</hi></head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                        type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1924-06-21">June 21, 1924</date>
                </dateline>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <p>The Ambassador said that he had been instructed to suggest a project of a
                    protocol relating to the adhesion of Russia to the Spitzbergen Treaty. This
                    protocol was to the effect that notwithstanding the stipulation of Article X of
                    the Treaty Russia should be allowed to give her adhesion even though all the
                    high contracting parties had not recognized the Russian Government. The
                    Ambassador left with the Secretary a copy of this proposal.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d3-f1"/></p>
                <p>The Ambassador also left a memorandum giving a proposal of Mr. MacDonald. The
                    memorandum is as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Mr. Ramsay MacDonald has proposed, and we agree that, in the protocol
                            concerning Spitzbergen, wherever the word “Russie” appears, it be
                            replaced by the expression: “Union des Républiques sovietiques
                            socialistes”.</p></quote></p>
                <pb facs="0117" n="3" xml:id="pg3"/>
                <p>The Secretary said that he did not care to comment on the matter at present; that
                    it seemed to us that this question had been dealt with by Article X of the
                    Spitzbergen Treaty and that this Government could not amend that Article without
                    the consent of the Senate. The Secretary said that he would consider the
                    question whether the suggestion of the French Government amounted to an
                    amendment of the Article.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
